[38;2;124;124;124mIs the join of group topologies a group topology? The supremum of a family of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup topologies is again a group topology. This is because the group operations[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbeing continuous is preserved under taking suprema of topologies... [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, actually that's not obvious. Let me think more carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf τ₁ and τ₂ are group topologies on a group G, is their join τ₁ ∨ τ₂ (the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoarsest topology finer than both) a group topology?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe join of two group topologies is indeed a group topology. More generally, the[0m[38;2;124;124;124msupremum of any family of group topologies is a group topology. This is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstandard result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof sketch: The supremum of topologies {τ_i} is the topology generated by the [0m[38;2;124;124;124msubbasis ⋃_i τ_i. For the multiplication map m: G × G → G to be continuous, we [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mneed that for each subbasic open set U ∈ τ_i, m^{-1}(U) is open in G × G with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe product topology from τ₁ ∨ τ₂. But m^{-1}(U) is open in (G, τ_i) × (G, τ_i),[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand the product topology from τ₁ ∨ τ₂ is finer than the product topology from [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mτ_i, so m^{-1}(U) is open. Similarly for inversion.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the supremum of group topologies is a group topology. Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the set of all group topologies on F(X) making ι continuous is non-empty [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(the indiscrete topology always works). The supremum of all such topologies is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe finest group topology making ι continuous. Call it τ*.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThen (F(X), τ*) is a topological group, and ι: X → (F(X), τ*) is continuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow we verify the universal property. Given any topological group G and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuous map f: X → G, by the universal property of the free (abstract) group,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthere's a unique group homomorphism f': F(X) → G extending f. We need f' to be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe topology on G is a group topology, and f' is a group homomorphism. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpullback topology f'^{-1}(τ_G) on F(X) is a group topology (since f' is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhomomorphism and τ_G is a group topology). Moreover, ι: X → (F(X), f'^{-1}(τ_G))[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis continuous because f' ∘ ι = f is continuous, which means ι is continuous into[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe pullback topology.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo f'^{-1}(τ_G) is one of the group topologies making ι continuous, hence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mf'^{-1}(τ_G) ⊆ τ* (since τ* is the finest such). This means f': (F(X), τ*) → (G,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mτ_G) is continuous. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the universal property is satisfied, and (F(X), τ*) is the free topological [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup on X.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis works for ANY topological space X. So the answer is YES.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also note: if X is Tychonoff, then τ* is Hausdorff (this is Markov's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem). If X is not Tychonoff, τ* might not be Hausdorff.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is complete. The forgetful functor U: TopGrp → Top admits a left [0m
[38;2;124;124;124madjoint, given by the free topological group construction.[0m

